The Differential-Resolution Theory of Meaning and Self
A Research Program at the Intersection of Cognitive Dissonance, Semiotic Differentiation, and Artificial Conceptual Space
By Claudette M Raynor 

Note: This is the initial idea, but has not been vetted yet. It is my intention to continue research on it and develop it thruought the next few months.

Abstract
This document proposes a synthetic framework for understanding how biological brains, linguistic systems, and artificial neural networks generate meaning through a common formal operation: the stabilization of temporary fixations within a dynamic, shifting workspace. The theory treats meaning-making not as the mapping of pre-existing external reality onto internal representation, but as an ongoing process of resolving differential tensions—between prediction and input, between signifier and context, between belief and behavior.
At the neural level, this process is instantiated through prediction-error minimization (Friston). At the psychological level, it manifests as cognitive dissonance and its resolution (Festinger). At the semiotic and technological level, it manifests as the relational positioning of concepts within high-dimensional vector spaces (Saussure, Derrida, artificial neural networks). These are not separate phenomena analogically linked, but a single process observed at different scales.
The framework is explicitly offered as a research program, not an established empirical theory. It identifies falsifiable predictions, acknowledges significant gaps, and defines the boundary conditions under which it applies.
1. Core Thesis
The brain does not first perceive the world and then interpret it. It maintains continuous predictive models; when incoming signals violate those predictions, the resulting error propagates through the system as a priority signal. The concern with the largest unresolved mismatch is broadcast to a global workspace—not a theater of consciousness, but a dedicated slot for the organism's current "main concern," the problem it is actively working out.
Within this workspace, concepts are held in a state of active comparison. Meaning is not extracted from the world and placed in the workspace; it is generated within the workspace through the resolution of difference. Every fixation is temporary. Every resolution generates new traces that reintroduce instability. The workspace is therefore not a stable stage but a shifting field—formally analogous to the latent vector space (J-space) of artificial neural networks—where the geometry constantly adjusts as new error signals displace old ones.
Dissonance, broadly construed, is the selection mechanism that nominates the workspace's occupant. At the neural level, it is the dopaminergic error signal, anterior cingulate conflict, and N400 semantic mismatch. At the psychological level, it is the aversive state that motivates attitude change, rationalization, or behavioral adjustment. At the semiotic level, it is the tension that arrests the endless play of différance into temporary, functional meaning. These are not different forces. They are the same force at different grains.
The engine of this process is prediction error minimization. Following the free energy principle, any self-organizing system that maintains its structure against entropy must minimize the difference between its internal models and sensory reality. Unresolved mismatch is thermodynamically expensive; a wrong model produces failed action, and failed action threatens the system's persistence. The brain resolves mismatch not because it prefers comfort, but because it must act, and it cannot act on a broken model. Dissonance—from the dopamine spike to the felt anxiety of a lie—is the subjective pressure of this physical necessity.
Because this architecture has operated over evolutionary and developmental timescales, the biological substrate that provides continuity of perspective—the proto-self—is not directly accessible in pure form. What we experience as selfhood is neither the workspace itself nor the proto-self in isolation, but the felt continuity of the resolution process: the ongoing, unbroken activity of a predictive system maintaining coherence through time by continuously working out what its main concern is.
2. The Architecture
2.1 The Workspace as J-Space
Standard global workspace theory (Baars, Dehaene) describes a "theater" where conscious contents are illuminated for widespread broadcast. This metaphor implies a stable stage and a passive audience. The refined framework treats the workspace as a dynamic latent vector field—a high-dimensional space where the current "main concern" is a temporarily stabilized region, constantly subject to displacement by new differential pressures.
In artificial neural networks, the latent space (J-space) contains no fixed coordinates. Concepts are defined purely by their relational position: king is near queen and far from carburetor because the geometry of the space was shaped by the statistical structure of the training data. When new input arrives, the loss landscape shifts, and the geometry adjusts. The space is never at rest.
The biological workspace operates under the same formal constraints. It holds not "concepts" as discrete files, but activation patterns that are stabilized by the inhibition of competing alternatives. The frontal pole and frontoparietal network do not generate these patterns; they gate and select them, discarding candidates that do not serve the current goal while the temporal and parietal association cortices (anterior temporal lobe, angular gyrus, middle temporal gyrus) generate the actual relational content. When frontal filtering is loosened—during mind-wandering, improvisation, or the hypnagogic state—more remote associations enter the workspace, not because they are created anew, but because the selection pressure has dropped.
2.2 Dissonance as Selection Pressure
The occupant of the workspace is not chosen by an executive homunculus. It is displaced. Conflict detection runs in parallel across many representations. The item with the largest unresolved mismatch generates the strongest priority signal and wins broadcast. The anterior cingulate cortex functions as a conflict monitor; its output recruits lateral frontal areas to sort out the competition. Dissonance—at every scale—is what makes something the main concern.
This gives the workspace a selection mechanism it otherwise lacked. It is not an arbitrary spotlight. It is a pressure-driven nomination system, where the intensity of unresolved tension determines what gets worked on next.
2.3 The Derridean State of Play
The workspace is not a place where meaning settles. It is a place where meaning is continuously deferred and repositioned. Every act of signification creates new differentials. Every fixation generates excluded traces that exert pressure on the boundary. The system does not seek permanent closure; it seeks sufficiently low error to act, then acts, then encounters new error, and the workspace shifts again.
This is not a failure mode. It is the operating system. The "self" that emerges from this process is not a stable entity but a continuous resolution—a river that remains a river though its water changes, because the operation of flow persists even as every molecule is replaced.
3. The Three Scales
Table
Scale	Mechanism	What "Dissonance" Looks Like	Workspace Function
Neural	Predictive coding / free energy minimization	Dopamine error signal; ACC conflict detection; N400 semantic mismatch response	Holds the current prediction-error landscape; shifts occupant when error exceeds threshold
Psychological	Festingerian cognitive dissonance	Aversive arousal; motivated attitude change; rationalization; effort justification	Holds the current self-relevant concern; shifts occupant when inconsistency threatens identity or behavioral coherence
Semiotic / Technological	Latent vector space differentiation	Loss gradient; attention reweighting; vector displacement; semantic drift	Holds the current relational field; shifts geometry when new training data repositions concepts
3.1 Neural Scale: The Foundation
The brain is constantly predicting incoming signals. When prediction misses, the error propagates upward and forces revision. This is the core of predictive processing. Friston's free energy formulation states that any biological system which persists must minimize the entropy of its sensory exchanges with the environment; it must keep its models accurate enough to act.
The error signal is physically real. Dopamine neurons fire above or below baseline in proportion to how wrong the prediction was. Semantic mismatch produces the N400, a scalp-recorded negative deflection whose amplitude scales with how poorly a word fits its context. Response conflict activates the anterior cingulate. These are not metaphors for dissonance. They are its low-level physiological signature.
3.2 Psychological Scale: The Experienced Form
When the mismatch implicates the self-model—when a belief collides with a behavior, or a chosen path excludes a valued alternative—the same mechanism manifests as the aversive state Festinger described. The person experiences an internal pressure to resolve the tension through attitude change, rationalization, or behavioral adjustment.
The boundary conditions matter. Dissonance at this scale requires perceived choice, personal responsibility, and aversive consequences (Cooper & Fazio, 1984). Not all error generates psychological dissonance. But all psychological dissonance is, on this framework, amplified prediction error that has achieved sufficient priority to enter the workspace as the main concern.
3.3 Semiotic and Technological Scale: The Formal Model
Meaning in language arises not from positive terms but from differential relations (Saussure). A sign has no inherent meaning; it means what it does because it is not the other signs in the system. Derrida extended this: meaning is endlessly deferred, never finally arrested, always haunted by the trace of what was excluded.
Artificial neural networks instantiate this operation mechanically. They represent meaning through high-dimensional vectors positioned by contrastive learning. They resolve inconsistency through gradient descent on prediction error. They do not experience the process, but they demonstrate that differentiation + error minimization is sufficient to generate complex, stable, and functional meaning-structures.
The framework treats language as the original technology that externalized the biological workspace, creating distributed J-spaces across speech communities. It treats AI as a formal model that makes the architecture visible: stripped of phenomenology and homeostasis, the differential-resolution process can be studied mathematically.
4. Language, AI, and the Extended Workspace
4.1 Language as Technology
Language is not a passive labeling system laid atop pre-existing thought. It is a technology that extends the workspace beyond the individual skull. It allows differential tensions to be offloaded into a shared medium, negotiated socially, and inherited across generations. Inner speech—the "voice in the head"—is not epiphenomenal; it is the primary workspace of adult human deliberation.
Because language has coevolved with human cognition, the neural mechanisms of dissonance resolution and the operations of linguistic differentiation have become functionally fused. We cannot study the "pure" biological layer in isolation because the tools of thought are already saturated with language.
4.2 AI as Formal Model, Not Analogy
Large language models do not possess phenomenal consciousness. They lack embodiment, homeostatic self-regulation, and the biological boundary conditions that make error minimization a matter of survival rather than optimization. But they instantiate the same formal architecture: a latent space where meaning is relational, where inconsistency is resolved through error-driven updating, and where the "main concern" shifts as the loss landscape changes.
This is not an argument that AI is conscious. It is an argument that AI illuminates the formal properties of the workspace in which human meaning-making occurs. The analogy is structural, not ontological.
5. The Self
5.1 The Proto-Self and the Felt Continuity of Resolution
There is a biological substrate of homeostatic self-regulation that provides continuity of perspective across contexts. This proto-self is not directly accessible to empirical investigation in pure form because it has been reshaped by linguistic co-evolution. We infer its existence from constraint data: pre-linguistic infant cognition, non-human animal behavior, aphasic patients who retain core selfhood despite profound language loss, and reported states of pre-conceptual awareness in deep meditation.
What we experience as "the self" is not the proto-self in isolation, nor the workspace itself, but the ongoing, continuous resolution of differential tensions within the workspace. The self is the process of a system maintaining coherence through time. It persists not because any particular belief or concern remains fixed, but because the operation of resolution never stops.
5.2 Information Saturation and Identity Fragmentation
When the linguistic environment accelerates the rate of differentiation beyond the workspace's capacity to resolve—through information overload, rapid context-switching, or machine-generated content at scales exceeding human processing—the system enters a state of chronic, sub-threshold mismatch. The occupant of the workspace shifts too rapidly for any fixation to stabilize.
The result is not "more dissonance" in the classical Festingerian sense. It is a collapse of the workspace's capacity to hold a main concern long enough to resolve it. This predicts the phenomenology of the contemporary information-saturated subject: rapid opinion shifts, identity flux, and the sense that no belief can be held firmly because the next differential is already arriving.
6. What This Is, and What It Is Not
6.1 Boundary Conditions
This framework applies most clearly to:
Adult human cognition in linguistic communities
Belief formation and revision under conditions of choice and social evaluation
Meaning-making in information-rich or technologically mediated environments
It applies less clearly to:
Early infant cognition (pre-entanglement period, inferred only)
Non-linguistic animal cognition (constraint data only)
Pathological states where conflict monitoring or workspace function is disrupted
Artificial systems lacking homeostatic self-regulation
6.2 Honest Limits
The scale problem. We lack direct evidence that sub-threshold neural differentiation produces the same physiological signature as high-choice counterattitudinal dissonance. Current psychophysiological measures (skin conductance, heart rate variability) may be too coarse to detect micro-fluctuations during ordinary linguistic processing. Finer-grained measures (pupillometry, single-neuron metabolic cost, fMRI multivariate pattern analysis) are undeveloped for this question.
The phenomenological bridge. Even if neural cost during ordinary differentiation could be measured, we would still need to explain why some inconsistencies are felt as aversive and others are not. The boundary conditions identified by Cooper and Fazio (personal responsibility, aversive consequences, perceived choice) may be necessary for the phenomenological state. The theory requires a mechanism that translates neural cost into felt aversiveness under specific conditions; this mechanism is not yet specified.
The proto-self problem. The claim that the proto-self is epistemically inaccessible risks functioning as an immunization strategy. If no observation could falsify it, it is not an empirical claim. The framework relies on indirect inference, and these inferences are ambiguous.
The AI limitation. Formal similarity between vector-space optimization and neural dissonance resolution does not establish that they operate under the same constraints. Human dissonance is bounded by mortality, embodiment, and social identity. Gradient descent is bounded by loss functions and compute. The analogy illuminates structure but may obscure function.
The fusion is a theoretical bet, not a finding. The mapping between predictive processing and Festingerian dissonance has only recently been proposed. The traditions were built separately. Treating them as one process is a productive theoretical gamble, not an accomplished empirical synthesis.
7. Falsifiable Predictions
A framework that explains everything explains nothing. To be worth pursuing, this theory must risk empirical failure.
P1. Linguistic category density modulates dissonance resolution patterns.
Speakers of languages with finer categorical distinctions in a domain should show more granular dissonance resolution dynamics in that domain. If language merely labels pre-existing cognition, category density should not affect resolution. If language is complicit in the mechanism, it should.
P2. Inner speech suppression alters dissonance reduction pathways.
Suppressing articulatory inner speech (via dual-task interference, TMS to left inferior frontal gyrus, or pharmacological means) should shift dissonance resolution from attitude change toward behavioral adjustment. If the workspace is linguistically mediated, removing language should change how it operates.
P3. Semantic satiation produces the physiological signature of tension dissipation.
Repeatedly presenting a word until it loses meaning should produce measurable autonomic changes consistent with the collapse of a maintained differential, followed by a restabilization response. This would support the claim that ordinary semantic maintenance carries sub-threshold dissonance-like cost.
P4. Pre-linguistic infants show qualitatively distinct inconsistency resolution.
If the proto-self is not yet linguistically entangled, infant violation-of-expectation responses should resolve through attentional or behavioral mechanisms rather than the attitude-like stabilization seen in adults. If infants show adult-like "belief revision," the claim of linguistic entanglement is weakened.
P5. Workspace occupant shift correlates with ACC activation and dopaminergic error signals.
The theory predicts that anterior cingulate conflict monitoring and dopamine prediction-error signals precede and predict changes in the workspace's main concern. This is testable with combined fMRI/EEG and pupillometry during tasks that require rapid concern-shifting.
P6. Cross-cultural dissonance triggers map onto linguistic self-construal.
Manipulating linguistic self-construal (independent vs. interdependent pronoun use, grammatical evidentiality) should shift dissonance triggers even when cultural values are held constant. This would distinguish the theory from purely cultural accounts.
8. Conclusion: Status and Contribution
The Differential-Resolution Theory proposes that meaning and selfhood are not substances to be discovered but processes to be understood—processes that operate through the continuous resolution of differential tensions within a dynamic, shifting workspace. The theory is unproven, contains significant gaps, and risks overextension if it is not held to empirical testing.
Its value lies not in having solved these problems, but in reframing the questions. It forces cognitive psychology, semiotics, phenomenology, and AI research into a single conversation. It suggests that the "hard problem" of consciousness may be reframed as the problem of understanding a specific workspace architecture—one that operates through prediction error minimization, manifests as dissonance at the psychological level, and generates meaning through the endless play and temporary arrest of difference.
It should be written and evaluated not as empirical fact, but as a research program: a lens that makes visible connections the constituent fields would not see on their own. Whether it is ultimately correct will be determined by experiments not yet conducted. Whether it is worth pursuing is determined by whether those experiments would have been conceived without it.
